Three edits to this one file. Line numbers below are this checkout's (07406d2) BEFORE the change; everything below shifts down once edit (a) lands above the describe.

(a) Fetch re-arm. Hoist the baseURL template literal out of the beforeAll at line 16 to a module-scope `const baseURL = `${__dirname}/../../../../../../node_modules/@jlongster/sql.js/dist/`;` declared above the beforeAll, leaving the beforeAll as `patchFetchForSqlJS(baseURL); return init({ baseURL });` with its blank line. Add a top-level beforeEach directly after the beforeAll containing `patchFetchForSqlJS(baseURL);`, preceded by a comment saying: the afterEach below calls vi.restoreAllMocks(), which is file-scoped rather than spy-scoped, so it takes down the global.fetch spy this file's beforeAll installed along with the console spies; nothing breaks today because init() has already run and sql.js caches the compiled wasm, but from the second test on global.fetch is the real jsdom fetch; patchFetchForSqlJS is a vi.spyOn(...).mockImplementation(...) with no restore of its own, so calling it again is idempotent. Leave the afterEach at line 35 exactly as it is — the fix re-arms the patch, it does not narrow the restore.

(b) Rewrite the comment at lines 28-31 (the block beginning 'Teardown lives here rather than at the tail of a test body' and ending 'on both halves — restore mocks, then close handles'). It over-claims on both halves, and every count in the replacement must be true of the describe as it will exist AFTER edit (c) adds a tenth test to it — that is the defect this work order was rejected for previously. Replace it with exactly this text, wrapped to 80 columns to match the file's printWidth and its habit of naming tests by title rather than by position:

  // Teardown lives here rather than at the tail of a test body: an assertion
  // failing above it would skip the cleanup. What survives that is narrower
  // than it looks, so each half is scoped to what it actually covers.
  //
  // vi.restoreAllMocks() restores every spy in the file. Five of the ten tests
  // mock console.log — the three transaction tests, the once-per-pattern test
  // and the once-per-handle test — and each of those restores its own spy at
  // the tail of its body anyway; this is the backstop for an assertion that
  // fails before it gets there. The other five mock no console.log, so for them
  // there is nothing to restore.
  //
  // The loop closes the handles that were pushed into it, and exactly one test
  // pushes any: 'should report an unparseable pattern once per database handle,
  // not once per process'. Eight of the other nine call openDatabase() without
  // registering the handle, so those are untouched either way; the fetch-patch
  // test at the end opens no database at all.
  //
  // Mirrors ./index.electron.test.ts on both halves — restore mocks, then
  // close handles — where each of that file's three tests registers both.

Where each number comes from, all re-derived on this checkout and re-confirmed after applying the change: ten tests exist after edit (c); five of them mock console.log (the it() blocks currently opening at 41, 70, 110, 260 and 325, whose `spyOn(console` sites are 52, 87, 131, 282 and 350); the other five — the crdt-index test, the two regex-matching tests, the valid-regex test and the new fetch-patch test — mock no console.log; exactly one pushes handles (the block currently opening at 325, via `handles.push` at 342 and 345); and of the nine tests other than that one, eight call openDatabase() without registering (currently 42, 71, 111, 154, 199, 220, 261, 382) while the new fetch-patch test calls openDatabase() not at all.

Requirements on this comment, which AC-4 checks mechanically: it must not contain the literal 'every test', 'every handle', 'the last test' or 'the final test'. In particular the closing Mirrors sentence above deliberately says 'each of that file's three tests' and NOT 'every test' — index.electron.test.ts genuinely does have all three of its tests register both a console mock and a handle (53/55, 89 with 83/86, 139 with 141/146), but a literal-substring check cannot tell a true claim about that other file from the in-file over-claim this ticket exists to remove, so the wording carries no banned phrase. It must not attribute the handle claim to the test currently opening at line 381 ('should still match a valid regex that does match, and not throw on one that does not'), which never pushes. Name tests by title.

(c) Append a final it(...) at the end of the describe block, named 'keeps the sql.js wasm fetch patched for every test, not just the first', whose body is a single `expect(vi.isMockFunction(globalThis.fetch)).toBe(true)`. Precede it with a comment saying it is deliberately last and why, in three clauses: a fetch assertion placed first would still see the beforeAll's patch because no afterEach has run yet — measured on this checkout, where a first-positioned probe prints isMock=true with the defect present and a last-positioned probe prints isMock=false — so it would pass for the wrong reason; and it would also be vacuous whenever `-t` selects it alone, which I measured too (with the beforeEach removed, `vitest --run -t 'keeps the sql.js wasm fetch patched'` still reports 'Tests 1 passed | 15 skipped (16)', because the beforeAll patch is still standing). This mirrors the convention argued at index.electron.test.ts:129-137.

Write the arrow body on one line (`it('...', () => expect(vi.isMockFunction(globalThis.fetch)).toBe(true));`); oxfmt collapses a wrapped arrow-body expression, and `yarn lint` fails on the check if you write it wrapped without running `yarn lint:fix` afterwards.
